\title{Cohomological Obstructions to Patching Incomplete Data}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Incomplete data are often summarized by a scalar missing rate, but coherent reconstruction depends on the incidence of local observations and the ranks of their restriction maps. We model local observations, pairwise residuals, and triple-overlap checks by a finite cochain complex
\[
C^0\xrightarrow{d^0}C^1\xrightarrow{d^1}C^2,\qquad d^1d^0=0.
\]
Here $H^0=\ker d^0$ contains globally compatible observations and $H^1=\ker d^1/\operatorname{im}d^0$ contains locally consistent residuals that no local correction removes. We establish the exact formula
\[
\dim H^1=\dim C^1-\operatorname{rank}d^0-\operatorname{rank}d^1,
\]
characterize vanishing by exactness, and derive sharp maximal and vanishing boundary cases. Thus neither overlap dimension nor missing rate alone determines the obstruction. We also prove that a flag data nerve is recovered exactly from its pairwise-overlap graph. Matrix algorithms, examples, statistical limitations, and directions toward random and spectral models are presented.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A dataset assembled from partial records is inherently local. One record may cover features $A$ and $B$, another $B$ and $C$, and another $A$ and $C$. Their compatibility is tested on overlaps. Pairwise agreement is necessary for a global reconstruction, but it need not be sufficient: discrepancies may circulate around cycles, and pairwise intersections do not always encode genuine higher intersections.

This is a gluing problem. Local objects are given on overlapping regions, restriction maps compare them, and one asks whether compatible local data arise from a global object. Cohomology isolates the obstruction. In the finite-dimensional linear setting considered here, the mechanism is explicit linear algebra while retaining its topological meaning.

The principal lesson is that missingness is not characterized by quantity alone. Equal numbers of missing entries can yield different overlap networks. Even when the overlap space has fixed dimension, restriction maps can make every residual obstructed or every residual patchable. A structural calculation must retain both combinatorial incidence and algebraic rank.

We introduce finite data complexes, prove an information-loss formula, characterize exact patchability, and give boundary examples proving non-identifiability from coarse summaries. We then identify flagness as the exact condition under which pairwise overlap data reconstruct all higher faces. The conclusions are deterministic; likelihood claims and missing-rate asymptotics require additional probabilistic assumptions.

\section{The data complex}

Let $\Bbbk$ be a field, typically $\mathbb R$ or $\mathbb C$.

\begin{definition}[Finite data complex]
A finite data complex consists of finite-dimensional $\Bbbk$-vector spaces $C^0,C^1,C^2$ and linear maps
\[
d^0:C^0\to C^1,\qquad d^1:C^1\to C^2
\]
satisfying $d^1\circ d^0=0$.
\end{definition}

The space $C^0$ contains local observations or corrections, $C^1$ contains residuals on pairwise overlaps, and $C^2$ contains consistency checks on triple overlaps or other second-order relations. The map $d^0$ computes discrepancies induced by local assignments. The map $d^1$ tests whether pairwise discrepancies are coherent around higher incidences. The equation $d^1d^0=0$ says that discrepancies produced by genuine local changes pass all higher consistency checks.

For a graph, assign a scalar $x_v$ to each vertex and orient each edge. Define $(d^0x)_{uv}=x_v-x_u$. Around an oriented triangle define $d^1y=y_{uv}+y_{vw}+y_{wu}$. The terms telescope, proving $d^1d^0=0$.

\begin{definition}[Zeroth and first cohomology]
The zeroth and first cohomology spaces are
\[
H^0=\ker d^0,\qquad H^1=\ker d^1/\operatorname{im}d^0.
\]
\end{definition}

Vectors in $H^0$ create no overlap discrepancy and represent globally compatible modes. A vector in $\ker d^1$ is a cocycle, a residual pattern passing every second-order test. A vector in $\operatorname{im}d^0$ is a coboundary, a residual explained by modifying local values. Thus $H^1$ records consistent residuals that cannot be patched. It measures the gap between passing local tests and admitting a global explanation.

\section{The information-loss formula}

\begin{lemma}[Coboundaries are cocycles]
For every finite data complex,
\[
\operatorname{im}d^0\subseteq\ker d^1.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $y=d^0x$, then $d^1y=d^1d^0x=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cohomological Information-Loss Formula]
For every finite data complex,
\[
\dim H^1+\operatorname{rank}d^0+\operatorname{rank}d^1=\dim C^1.
\]
Equivalently,
\[
\dim H^1=\dim C^1-\operatorname{rank}d^0-\operatorname{rank}d^1.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Rank--nullity applied to $d^1$ gives
\[
\dim\ker d^1=\dim C^1-\operatorname{rank}d^1.
\]
The preceding lemma places $\operatorname{im}d^0$ inside this kernel. The quotient dimension formula then gives
\begin{align*}
\dim H^1
&=\dim\ker d^1-\dim\operatorname{im}d^0\\
&=\dim C^1-\operatorname{rank}d^1-\operatorname{rank}d^0.
\end{align*}
\end{proof}

The formula partitions the overlap space into directions detected by $d^1$, directions removable through $d^0$, and directions surviving in cohomology. It also implies $\operatorname{rank}d^0+\operatorname{rank}d^1\leq\dim C^1$.

\begin{corollary}[Strict Rank-Deficit Criterion]
If
\[
\operatorname{rank}d^0+\operatorname{rank}d^1<\dim C^1,
\]
then $\dim H^1>0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The right-hand side of the information-loss formula is then strictly positive.
\end{proof}

This criterion detects a global obstruction using matrix ranks alone, without enumerating quotient classes.

\section{Exact patchability and sharp extremes}

\begin{theorem}[Exact Patchability Criterion]
For a finite data complex, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\dim H^1=0$;
\item $H^1$ is the zero vector space;
\item $\ker d^1=\operatorname{im}d^0$.
\end{enumerate}
Thus every locally consistent residual is generated by a local correction exactly when first cohomology vanishes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The quotient $\ker d^1/\operatorname{im}d^0$ is zero exactly when its denominator equals its numerator. In finite dimensions, a vector space has dimension zero exactly when it is the zero space.
\end{proof}

Vanishing of $H^1$ concerns existence of patches, not uniqueness. Nonzero $H^0=\ker d^0$ may allow different local assignments to produce the same residual.

\begin{theorem}[Surjective Patch Map]
If $d^0:C^0\to C^1$ is surjective, then $\dim H^1=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Surjectivity gives $\operatorname{im}d^0=C^1$. Since $d^1d^0=0$, the map $d^1$ vanishes on all of $C^1$. Thus $\ker d^1=C^1=\operatorname{im}d^0$, and exact patchability applies.
\end{proof}

Surjectivity is sufficient but not necessary: exactness only asks $\operatorname{im}d^0$ to fill $\ker d^1$.

\begin{theorem}[Maximal Obstruction for Zero Differentials]
If $d^0=0$ and $d^1=0$, then
\[
H^1\cong C^1,\qquad \dim H^1=\dim C^1.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The kernel of the zero second map is $C^1$, and the image of the zero first map is $\{0\}$. Hence $H^1=C^1/\{0\}\cong C^1$.
\end{proof}

No overlap residual is rejected in this regime, and none can be corrected.

\begin{theorem}[Non-identifiability from Overlap Dimension]
Consider two finite data complexes whose overlap spaces have the same positive dimension. If both maps in the first complex are zero and the first map in the second complex is surjective, then their first cohomology dimensions differ. The first equals the common overlap dimension, whereas the second equals zero.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the preceding two theorems. Positivity makes the resulting dimensions unequal.
\end{proof}

Consequently, no statistic determined only by $\dim C^1$ can determine obstruction dimension. A scalar missing rate carries even less structural information unless a generative model ties it to incidence and ranks. In particular, a universal deterministic law such as
\[
\dim H^1\approx r^2n\log(1/r)
\]
does not follow from the cochain framework. Such a law could only be assessed under a specified random model for records, overlaps, and restriction maps.

\section{The data nerve}

\begin{definition}[Data nerve]
Let $V$ index local charts. A data nerve is a simplicial complex $K$ on $V$: its faces are finite families of charts having genuine common overlap, and every subset of a face is again a face.
\end{definition}

The one-skeleton $K^{(1)}$ is the graph of pairwise overlaps. Given a graph $G$, its clique complex $\operatorname{Clique}(G)$ has as faces all finite vertex sets whose distinct pairs are edges.

\begin{definition}[Flag nerve]
A simplicial complex is flag if every clique in its one-skeleton is a face.
\end{definition}

Flagness is substantive. Three sets can overlap pairwise while having empty triple intersection; their nerve has three edges but no filled triangle and is not flag.

\begin{theorem}[Pairwise Compatibility Forms a Face]
Let $K$ be a flag data nerve. If every two distinct vertices in a finite set $S$ are adjacent in $K^{(1)}$, then $S$ is a face of $K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The hypothesis says that $S$ is a clique, and flagness says that every clique is a face.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Flag-Nerve Reconstruction]
If $K$ is a flag data nerve, then
\[
K=\operatorname{Clique}(K^{(1)}).
\]
Thus pairwise-overlap data recover the complete nerve.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every face of $K$ has all of its pairs as edges, so every face is a clique. Conversely, the previous theorem makes every clique a face. The two face collections coincide.
\end{proof}

This result identifies when graph data suffice. Without flagness, clique completion may add nonexistent higher overlaps, changing $C^2$, $d^1$, and ultimately $H^1$.

\section{Algorithms}

Choose bases and represent the maps by matrices $D_0$ and $D_1$. If $D_0$ is $m\times p$ and $D_1$ is $q\times m$, then $m=\dim C^1$ and the cochain condition is $D_1D_0=0$.

\subsection{Rank-based obstruction dimension}

The dimension algorithm proceeds as follows.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Verify that the matrix dimensions are compatible.
\item Check $D_1D_0=0$, exactly or within a declared numerical tolerance.
\item Compute $r_0=\operatorname{rank}D_0$ and $r_1=\operatorname{rank}D_1$.
\item Return $h_1=m-r_0-r_1$.
\end{enumerate}
Dense Gaussian elimination has cubic worst-case cost in the largest matrix dimension. Sparse structure can substantially reduce the cost.

\subsection{Obstruction representatives}

To exhibit classes, compute a column basis $B$ of $D_0$ and a null-space basis $Z$ of $D_1$. Initialize an accepted basis with the columns of $B$. Scan the columns of $Z$ and retain a column whenever it increases the rank of the accepted family. The retained columns represent a basis of $H^1$. Their number is
\[
\dim\ker D_1-\operatorname{rank}D_0=\dim H^1.
\]
Numerical implementations require a rank tolerance.

\subsection{Flag completion}

Given an undirected overlap graph, enumerate all cliques and declare them to be faces. This produces the unique flag complex with that one-skeleton. Clique enumeration is exponential in the worst case, necessarily so when the output has exponentially many faces. The construction recovers the actual nerve only when flagness is independently justified.

\section{Numerical examples}

\begin{example}[One independent obstruction]
Let
\[
D_0=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\\0&0\\0&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
D_1=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&1&0\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Then $D_1D_0=0$, $\operatorname{rank}D_0=2$, $\operatorname{rank}D_1=1$, and $\dim C^1=4$. Hence $\dim H^1=1$. The kernel of $D_1$ is spanned by the first, second, and fourth coordinate vectors. The first two are boundaries, while the fourth represents the surviving class.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Equal size, opposite patchability]
Take $C^1=\mathbb R^3$. If both maps are zero, then $\dim H^1=3$. If $C^0=\mathbb R^3$, $D_0=I_3$, and $D_1=0$, then $D_0$ is surjective and $\dim H^1=0$. The overlap dimensions agree while the obstruction dimensions are opposite extremes.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Graph cycle rank]
Regard a connected graph as a one-dimensional complex. Let $d^0$ be its oriented incidence matrix and let $d^1=0$. With $v$ vertices and $e$ edges, the incidence matrix has rank $v-1$, so
\[
\dim H^1=e-v+1.
\]
A tree has no obstruction, while a four-cycle has one. If a triangular clique is filled by a two-face, its boundary cycle is detected by $d^1$, illustrating how higher overlap data remove a would-be obstruction.
\end{example}

\section{Statistical interpretation and limitations}

Given an observed residual $y\in C^1$ and inner products, one may choose $x\in C^0$ minimizing
\[
\|d^0x-y\|^2.
\]
This projects $y$ onto $\operatorname{im}d^0$. When $y\in\ker d^1$, the unexplained component represents its cohomological obstruction. This is a natural least-squares operation, but it is not automatically maximum likelihood. That interpretation requires an explicit noise law, such as Gaussian residual noise with specified covariance. A posterior mean further requires a prior and careful treatment of $H^0$.

Nor do the deterministic results establish that a cohomological procedure outperforms mean, nearest-neighbor, or chained-equation imputation. A valid comparison requires a common data-generating distribution, missingness mechanism, hyperparameters, and loss function. Cohomological quantities instead provide structural predictors and impossibility diagnostics for such studies.

A missing rate $r$ should be treated as a parameter of a random model, not as a sufficient statistic for topology. One model may link $r$ to edge and face probabilities in a random nerve, while separate assumptions control map ranks. The information-loss formula shows that both random incidence and algebraic cancellation matter.

\section{Discussion}

The exact dimension identity separates overlap directions into three classes: inconsistent directions detected by $d^1$, correctable directions generated by $d^0$, and genuine obstruction directions represented by $H^1$. This prevents conflating local consistency with global reconstructibility.

The extreme cases show that counting pairwise comparisons is insufficient. Equal-dimensional overlap spaces can differ maximally because dimensions do not record whether residuals are independent, detectable, or correctable. The flag theorem adds a combinatorial warning: pairwise overlaps determine higher intersections only under a structural assumption.

A sound workflow therefore has two layers. First construct the incidence structure and decide whether flag completion is appropriate. Then construct restriction maps and compute ranks or spectra. Neither layer replaces the other.

\section{Future directions}

A random-nerve theory should ask whether normalized first cohomology has a sharp threshold under Bernoulli missingness. The exact formula indicates that cycle creation and random map rank must be tracked together.

A two-parameter asymptotic law may use the cycle rank of the overlap nerve and the expected rank defect of restriction maps. The maximal and vanishing examples provide exact calibration points.

For noisy data, exact rank should be replaced by spectral information. Counting singular values below a tolerance gives an approximate obstruction dimension, while perturbation bounds based on spectral gaps may separate stable holes from numerical near-dependence.

Under an explicit Gaussian model, least squares can be related to posterior estimation. Zero eigenvalues of a sheaf Laplacian should capture irreducible cohomological risk, while small positive eigenvalues quantify instability. Empirical work can then test whether these summaries predict imputation performance beyond missing rate alone.

\section{Conclusion}

Incomplete data have both an amount and an arrangement. The arrangement is encoded by an overlap nerve and by linear maps comparing local observations. In a finite data complex,
\[
\dim H^1=\dim C^1-\operatorname{rank}d^0-\operatorname{rank}d^1.
\]
First cohomology vanishes exactly when every locally consistent residual comes from a local correction. A surjective patch map guarantees vanishing; zero differentials produce maximal obstruction. Thus scalar missingness cannot determine repairability without incidence and rank assumptions. When the nerve is flag, pairwise overlap data recover all higher faces. Together these results give a precise structural account of what consistency rejects, what correction repairs, and what survives as genuinely missing information.

\end{document}

